INT. HOUSEBOAT, DINING TABLE - NIGHT

A narrow cabin lit by a hurricane lamp. A pot of stew steams. The hull knocks gently against a pier.

ROSALEEN FITZPATRICK, sixty, her hair pinned up, ladles stew. Her husband THADDEUS FITZPATRICK, sixty-two, bearded, tears bread in silence. Their son CILLIAN, thirty-four, in a city jacket, stares at his bowl. And his wife YASMIN FITZPATRICK, thirty, folds her napkin into smaller and smaller squares.

ROSALEEN
Eat.

CILLIAN
I'm not hungry.

ROSALEEN
You drove four hours.

CILLIAN
I had a sandwich.

THADDEUS
Where?

CILLIAN
A service station.

THADDEUS
They'll charge you three pounds for a sandwich.

CILLIAN
It was four.

THADDEUS
Robbery.

ROSALEEN
Yasmin, more stew?

YASMIN
I'm fine, thank you.

ROSALEEN
You didn't finish.

YASMIN
I'm full.

ROSALEEN
You're thin.

YASMIN
I'm what I always am.

A pause. Thaddeus sets down the bread.

THADDEUS
Your mother made this stew.

CILLIAN
It's good.

THADDEUS
It's the same as last year.

CILLIAN
That's what makes it good.

THADDEUS
Then why do you look like that?

CILLIAN
Like what?

THADDEUS
Like a man who's sold the boat.

Silence. Rosaleen sets down the ladle. Yasmin's napkin is a tiny white square.

CILLIAN
Dad.

THADDEUS
Did you sell the boat?

CILLIAN
I haven't sold anything.

THADDEUS
You've had a man to look at her.

CILLIAN
That was a surveyor. For the bank.

THADDEUS
The bank.

ROSALEEN
Thaddeus.

THADDEUS
The bank, Rosaleen.

CILLIAN
The mortgage is three months behind. I didn't know how to say it.

THADDEUS
So you brought a surveyor.

CILLIAN
I brought help.

THADDEUS
I don't need help.

He rises. The chair scrapes, the lamp swings, shadows slide across the walls. He goes up the stairs and out onto the deck, and the hull rocks under his boots. Nobody moves.
